{{Short description|Soup native to the Efiks in Nigeria}}
{{Use Nigerian English|date=May 2026}}
[[File:Edikang ikong.jpg|thumb|right|Edikang ikong]]

'''Edikang ikong''' is a [[vegetable soup]] made with [[Telfairia occidentalis|ikong ubong]] that originated among the [[Efik people]] of [[Cross River State]] and Ibibio people of [[Akwa Ibom State]] in [[Nigeria]]<ref name="Pulse 2015"/><ref name="Ukpong 2016"/><ref>{{Cite news |date=2018-05-24 |title=Prepare Edikang Ikong With These 10 Easy Steps |url=https://guardian.ng/life/prepare-edikang-ikong-with-these-10-easy-steps/ |location=Lagos, Nigeria|access-date=2022-07-02 |newspaper=[[The Guardian (Nigeria)|The Guardian]]|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Online |first=Tribune |date=2019-12-28 |title=Why not delightsome Edikang-ikong soup? |url=https://tribuneonlineng.com/why-not-delightsome-edikang-ikong-soup/ |access-date=2022-07-02 |newspaper=[[Nigerian Tribune]]|language=en-GB}}</ref> It is considered to be a [[delicacy]] among some Nigerians, and is sometimes served during occasions of importance.<ref name="Iyobebe 2016"/><ref name="Agbenson 2014"/> Edikang ikong is a nutritious soup and expensive to prepare, and has been described as being mostly eaten by rich people in [[Nigeria]].<ref name="Iyobebe 2016"/> Ingredients used in edikang ikong include [[beef]] and [[dried fish]], [[bush meat]], [[crayfish]], shaki ([[Tripe|cow tripe]]), kanda, [[pumpkin]] leaves, water leaves, [[Telfairia occidentalis|ikong ubong]], [[onion]], [[Common periwinkle|periwinkle]], [[palm oil]], [[Edible salt|salt]] and [[black pepper|pepper]].<ref name="Pulse 2015"/><ref name="Iyobebe 2016"/><ref name="Inyese 2015"/><ref>{{Cite news |date=2018-03-26 |title=Edikang Ikong Recipe For Dummies |url=https://guardian.ng/life/food/edikang-ikong-recipe-for-dummies/ |location=Lagos, Nigeria|access-date=2022-07-02 |newspaper=[[The Guardian (Nigeria)|The Guardian]]|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=joan |date=2015-05-20 |title=Recipe for an amazing Edikang Ikong Soup |url=https://www.pulse.ng/lifestyle/food-travel/trad-perfect-recipe-for-an-amazing-edikang-ikong-soup/y9rk2dl |access-date=2022-07-02 |newspaper=[[Pulse Nigeria]]|archive-date=2022-07-02 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220702230247/https://www.pulse.ng/lifestyle/food-travel/trad-perfect-recipe-for-an-amazing-edikang-ikong-soup/y9rk2dl |url-status=dead }}</ref>

After preparation, edikang ikong is typically served with [[fufu]], [[wheat flour]], [[eba]], or [[pounded yam]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://leadership.ng/2018/08/25/edikaiakong-soup/|title=Edikaiakong Soup|date=2018-08-25|newspaper=[[Leadership (newspaper)|Leadership]]|language=en-GB|access-date=2019-04-19|archive-date=2019-04-19|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190419235325/https://leadership.ng/2018/08/25/edikaiakong-soup/|url-status=dead}}</ref>
